"Path: /Users/siddhartha/dev/StockTest/research/STRATEGY_SEARCH.md (562 lines; all 7 sections written; section 1 has the marked placeholder \"FINAL RECOMMENDATION - pending round 2 + judges\" for the lead to fill)\n\nSummary:\n1. Nothing in 13,778 configurations across 13 families clears the 7-item good-confidence bar in CA or FED, so the default is still an S&P 500 fund bought and held (IVV or VOO vs SPY: +0.03 to +0.04 pp/yr).\n2. Methods (section 2): 95 quarterly starts, 3- to 20-year windows, CA, FED and NONE regimes, 1986-2026 fund proxies and a 1930-2026 simulator. Parity with the site through `validate_lab.py`: 36 comparisons, worst difference 0.0. Verification added about 3,000 variants.\n3. Section 3 has one row per family with its most telling number, e.g. timing: 0 of 662 rules beat SPY in any 10-year window starting 2009 or later; TAA: 47 configs positive before tax, 0 after CA tax; stocks: 20 random picks \"beat\" SPY by +3.24 because of survivorship. A second table covers the 10 finalists that failed verification.\n4. Tax-managed rotation (section 4.1): KX3 +0.99, KX1 +0.93, site preset +0.66 pp/yr score in CA. Momentum beats random picks by +0.7 to +0.9 (t 5.7 to 7.2). Without QQQ and XLK the scores are -0.26 and +0.12; DSR is 0.04 to 0.05; KX3 minus the preset is +0.28 (p 0.38).\n5. The January 2000 start is an artifact: only SPY, MDY and DIA had enough history then. Scored from 2002 on, KX3 fails items 1-3. 2020-26 is -0.40, and the 2026 book is 54% growth/tech with 87% unrealized gain.\n6. Budget quirk confirmed in the site's own code (`backtester/composite.py`): the partial gain sale is sized on the average gain but FIFO sells the cheapest lots first. KX3 overshot its budget in 18 of 25 years (up to 1.32x). Exact sizing changes KX3's 2000-start full excess from +1.84 to +0.31 and its p from 0.011 to 0.40; the 95-window score barely moves.\n7. 2x SMA175/3% trend rule (section 4.2), CA score: +4.94 on 2000-26 (p 0.077), +4.62 on 1986-26 (p 0.067), +2.95 on 1930-85 (p 0.27). Max drawdowns -47% / -47% / -67% vs the index's -55% / -55% / -81%. DSR 0.0035, so it is conditional, not a pass.\n8. 3x was rejected: drawdowns of -88% (1930-85 windows) and -93% peak to trough from 1929-09; a 17-24% chance of an 80%+ drawdown in 20 years. Its survival of 1987 depended on selling at one day's close (with a one-day lag the drawdown is -78%), and the rule was picked after the fact (DSR 0.013).\n9. Lessons (section 5): taxes dominate; holding SPY forever already pays the minimum tax this engine allows; tax-managed execution lifts any picker (random +1.11 vs momentum +1.28); leverage is paid-for risk, worth only +1.5 to +2.6 at SPY-like volatility; good confidence needed an excess Sharpe of about 0.76 vs 0.32-0.47 found.\n10. Caveats (section 6): survivorship, synthetic leverage financing, distribution taxes, FIFO only, wash sales not enforced, same-close execution, no $3k offset. The VFINX distribution fix is documented (`VFINXR`, `long_r`), and section 7 maps every result file.\n\nNo `r2_*.md` reports existed when I finished, so round 2 is described as in progress. The `taa_models` and `sector_deep` reports were never written as .md; the appendix points to the `results_md` field in their r1 JSON instead."